<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
		<!-- Deploy wwwroot contents to the app's root, not _content/SpawnDev.WebTorrent/ -->
		<!-- Required for service worker scope — SW must be at app root to intercept all requests -->
		<StaticWebAssetBasePath Condition="$(StaticWebAssetBasePath) == ''">/</StaticWebAssetBasePath>
		<LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
		<Version>3.2.9-local.5</Version>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>v3.2.9-local.5: MaxWebConns default 4-&gt;1 (a web seed is a fallback HTTP source, not a CDN - one polite connection per client; with per-piece verify now cheap it saturates near the link anyway). Per-phase zero-copy verify/store profiling is now gated behind Torrent.EnableZcProfiling (default false) so production pays no Stopwatch interop. (Carries local.4 big-array single-read + local.2 bounded leaf-digest verify.) MEASURED end to end: cold read-driven load of a 313MB model 202.9s-&gt;35.4s (read phase 165.7s-&gt;2.1s), now network-bound (~25MB/s on one connection). v3.2.9-local.4: zero-copy v2 verify reads all leaf hashes in ONE JS-&gt;.NET marshal (Set each into a single Uint8Array JS-side, then one ReadBytes of the whole 8 KB) instead of a 32-byte ReadBytes per leaf - profiling showed the per-leaf marshal dominated the download (~166s of a 203s load on a 313MB model). v3.2.9-local.3: adds diagnostic per-phase profiling of the zero-copy verify/store pipeline (Torrent.ZcFetchMs/ZcDigestFireMs/ZcDigestWaitMs/ZcReadMs/ZcTreeMs/ZcStoreMs + ZcPieces + ResetZcProfile) to find where per-piece download time goes. No behavior change. v3.2.9-local.2: zero-copy v2 (Merkle) piece verification now hashes the 16 KiB leaves in BOUNDED-CONCURRENCY batches (MaxConcurrentLeafDigests, default 32) instead of firing all ~256 of a 4 MB piece's leaf digests at once. With several pieces verifying concurrently that queued ~1024 SubtleCrypto ops + Promise-&gt;Task bridges on the single WASM thread, starving the download request loop (download fetched a batch fast, then stalled ~15-20s verifying before the next fetch). Hashes are byte-identical; only the dispatch is bounded. v3.2.9-local.1: TorrentReadStream implements SpawnDev.BlazorJS IJSReadStream - a browser consumer can read piece data while it stays in JS (a Uint8Array via ReadUint8ArrayAsync) instead of copying it into the .NET heap, e.g. streaming model weights straight into a GPU buffer via IBrowserMemoryBuffer.CopyFromJS (zero JS-&gt;.NET-&gt;GPU copy). CanReadSync=false in the browser (sync Read throws), true on desktop. Requires SpawnDev.BlazorJS 3.5.12+. v3.2.8 (stable): Zero-copy browser download + service-worker seek-while-downloading + OPFS store perf. (1) Zero-copy web-seed download: a single-file torrent on a browser OPFS store keeps each piece's bytes in JS end to end (browser fetch -> Uint8Array -> SubtleCrypto 16 KiB-leaf hashes -> .NET Merkle tree over only the 32-byte hashes -> OPFS write); piece data never enters the .NET/WASM heap, per-piece verification preserved, desktop unaffected. (2) Multi-file zero-copy web seed (the SW streaming unlock; ~35x download vs the .NET block path on Sintel). (3) SW media streaming now supports seek-while-downloading on live torrents (the moov-never-arrived stall fixed via Critical()->UpdateWires debounce; plus _critical leak-on-cancel, last-piece stranding, and FIFO/idle-drain fixes). (4) OPFS AsyncFSChunkStore perf: last-whole-piece read cache (one OPFS read per piece instead of 16-64x re-reads), PutAsync via HeapView (one pinned memcpy instead of ToArray()+byte[] interop serialize; requires SpawnDev.BlazorJS 3.5.11), concurrent 16 KiB leaf hashing (Task.WhenAll). (5) O(pieces^2) picker fix on the zero-copy path: the budget check now runs inside the rarest/sequential walk loops so the walk stops the instant fetches are back in flight (was an O(pieces) re-scan per piece completion -> ~2.3MB/s on a ~1700-piece model); MaxWebConns 8->4 (at/under the browser ~6/origin HTTP/1.1 cap). (6) Web-seed download concurrency: fixed parallel range-request pipeline (MaxWebConns) instead of the chicken-and-egg speed-based pipeline that stayed at ~1 in flight. Requires SpawnDev.RTC 1.1.10 (periodic re-announce at the tracker interval, fixes cold-start swarm growth) + SpawnDev.BlazorJS 3.5.11. v3.2.7: Piece thread-safety (per-Piece re-entrant lock serializes the 8 state mutators; fixed a multi-threaded download race NRE on large torrents). v3.2.6: TorrentFileInfo.ReadUint8ArrayAsync zero-copy JS-typed range read.</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>True</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<EmbedAllSources>true</EmbedAllSources>
		<DebugType>Embedded</DebugType>
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
		<Title>SpawnDev.WebTorrent</Title>
		<Authors>LostBeard</Authors>
		<Description>Pure C# BitTorrent/WebTorrent client. 18 BEPs including BEP 52 v2 (SHA-256 + Merkle + hybrid + pure-v2 end-to-end), real WebRTC P2P (browser + desktop via SpawnDev.RTC), DHT with mutable items (BEP 46 — AI agent communication), web seeds, media streaming with seeking via service worker, OPFS storage. Includes webtorrent-sw.js for COI headers + torrent streaming. No JavaScript dependencies. Runs on desktop (.NET + SIPSorcery) and browser (Blazor WASM + SpawnDev.BlazorJS). 468+ shared test methods running on both browser and desktop runtimes + libtorrent 2.0 external-interop fixtures.</Description>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.WebTorrent</PackageProjectUrl>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE.txt</PackageLicenseFile>
		<PackageIcon>icon-128.png</PackageIcon>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.WebTorrent.git</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageTags>BitTorrent;WebTorrent;P2P;Torrent;Blazor;WebAssembly;WebRTC;DHT;Streaming;MachineLearning;ModelDelivery;SIPSorcery</PackageTags>
		<NoPackageAnalysis>true</NoPackageAnalysis>
		<IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="SpawnDev.WebTorrent.Tests" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="SpawnDev.WebTorrent.Demo.Shared" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="Assets\icon-128.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\LICENSE.txt" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.BlazorJS" Version="3.5.12-local.1" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.AsyncFileSystem" Version="1.4.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.BlazorJS.Cryptography" Version="3.2.0" />
		<!-- SpawnDev.RTC replaces direct SipSorcery dependency - provides cross-platform WebRTC
		     with proven browser-interop SipSorcery fork (SRTP profile fix) bundled inside.
		     PackageReference (not ProjectReference) so CI / GitHub Pages builds work from a
		     standalone SpawnDev.WebTorrent checkout - they don't have the sibling RTC repo.
		     1.1.1 is the minimum version: 1.1.0 declared a dep on public SIPSorcery 10.0.4-pre
		     which doesn't exist on nuget.org; 1.1.1 bundles the fork DLLs inline (ILGPU pattern). -->
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.RTC" Version="1.1.10" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="windows" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="linux" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="macos" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
